reak-glass playbook lives at\n   930\t`docs/operations/break-glass-playbook.md`.
It documents the support-only\n   931\tbreak-glass workflow for time-bounded cro
ss-tenant access through\n   932\t`POST /v1/organizations/{org_id}/break-glass` 
and\n   933\t`POST /v1/admin/break-glass`, immutable audit evidence with\n   934
\t`elevated_access=true`, and the access-only (no credential mint) guarantee.\n 
 935\t\n   936\tThe playbook preserves the production boundary: customer, agent,
and CI traffic\n   937\tgoes through the Yalla API; Postgres owns source of trut
h; `yalla-worker`\n   938\tperforms provisioning; and Dokploy remains a private 
dependency. Customers must\n   939\tnever receive Dokploy API tokens.\n   940\t\
n   941\tThe artifact requires operator-managed runtime configuration in\n   942
\t`/etc/yalla/control-plane.env` or `YALLA_CONTROL_PLANE_ENV_FILE`; documents th
e\n   943\trequired redacted variables (`YALLA_API_BASE_URL`, `YALLA_ADMIN_API_K
EY`,\n   944\t`YALLA_BREAK_GLASS_TARGET_ORG_ID`); and pins exact verification co
mmands\n   945\tincluding `go test -run TestBreakGlass ./...`, the playbook stat
ic test,\n   946\t`go test ./...`, `go test -race ./...`, `go vet ./...`, and\n 
 947\t`scripts/verify.sh`.\n   948\t\n   949\tThe playbook explains expected `ya
lla.output.v1` / `yalla.error.v1` envelopes\n   950\twith `request_id`, `correla
tion_id`, and session `id`, plus failure recovery\n   951\tfor validation errors
, authorization errors, session revocation, worker\n   952\tpause/resume, and th
e prohibition on ad hoc write SQL or raw Dokploy repair\n   953\tcalls. It also 
documents the opt-in external live-Dokploy smoke test and states\n   954\tthat i
t must never run against production.\n   955\t\n   956\tCI pins the break-glass 
playbook with\n   957\t`go test ./internal/release/... -run TestBreakGlassPlaybo
okArtifact`.\n   958\t\n   959\t## TLS Termination and Proxy Header Trust\n   96
0\t\n   961\tThe Yalla control-plane API binary (`cmd/yalla-api`) deliberately\n
  962\tdoes NOT terminate TLS itself.\n   963\t**TLS is terminated at the operat
or\'s reverse proxy**\n   964\t(ingress controller, load balancer, or front-door
proxy), which\n   965\tforwards the request to the API over an internal network.
Keeping certificate material out of the API process\n   966\tnarrows the secret 
surface, leverages the proxy\'s vetted cipher\n   967\tsuite / HSTS / OCSP stapl
ing defaults, and lets operators rotate\n   968\tcertificates without an API res
tart. The posture is pinned by\n   969\t`internal/release/http_server_hardening_
static_test.go`; a regression\n   970\tin any one of the following is caught at 
build time:\n   971\t\n   972\t- **No in-process TLS.** The API binary MUST NOT 
call\n   973\t  `ListenAndServeTLS` or `ServeTLS`, and the constructed\n   974\t
 `*http.Server` MUST NOT set `TLSConfig` or `TLSNextProto`. Any\n   975\t  TLS-t
ermination shape inside the binary is rejected.\n   976\t- **Slowloris guard.** 
Every `*http.Server` literal in the API\n   977\t  binary MUST set a positive `R
eadHeaderTimeout`. The current value\n   978\t  is `10 * time.Second`. Removing 
the field, or initialising the\n   979\t  server with the zero value, is rejecte
d.\n   980\t- **Closed-set proxy header trust.** The rate limiter resolves the\n
  981\t  caller\'s identity through `ClientIP(r)` in\n   982\t  `internal/contro
lplane/httpapi/ratelimit.go`. The helper consults\n   983\t  only two forwarding
headers:\n   984\t  - `X-Forwarded-For` — the **first hop only** is taken so a f
orged\n   985\t    tail entry cannot displace the real client.\n   986\t  - `X-R
eal-IP` as a fallback.\n   987\t  No other forwarded-* family header is honoured
. RFC 7239\n   988\t  `Forwarded`, Akamai `True-Client-IP`, Cloudflare\n   989\t
 `CF-Connecting-IP`, Fastly `Fastly-Client-IP`, `X-Client-IP`,\n   990\t  `X-Ori
ginal-Forwarded-For`, and the meta-data variants\n   991\t  (`X-Forwarded-Host`,
`X-Forwarded-Proto`, `X-Forwarded-Port`,\n   992\t  `X-Forwarded-Server`) are de
liberately ignored. If the API is\n   993\t  ever reachable directly — a network
misconfiguration, an internal\n   994\t  pivot, or a forgotten test fixture — an
attacker who can reach it\n   995\t  must not be able to spoof their identity pa
st the IP bucket by\n   996\t  sending one of those headers. The closed set is e
nforced by the\n   997\t  static analyser; adding a new header to the trust list
requires\n   998\t  an explicit, reviewed change to the allow-list.\n   999\t\n 
